# 🔐 Authentication & Role-Based Access

The application uses **Supabase Authentication** for user authentication.

Each authenticated user is associated with an application profile containing a role:

```text
admin
teacher
student
```

The role determines which portal and functionality the user can access.

### Role Flow

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A[User Login] --> B[Supabase Auth]
    B --> C[Authenticated User]
    C --> D[Profile / Role]
    
    D -->|admin| E[Administrator Portal]
    D -->|teacher| F[Teacher Portal]
    D -->|student| G[Student Portal]
    
    E --> H[School-wide Management]
    F --> I[Assigned Classes & Subjects]
    G --> J[Enrolled Section Only]
```

---

# 🏗️ System Architecture

The project follows a client-server architecture in which the React frontend communicates with Supabase services.

```mermaid
flowchart TB
    U[User / Browser]

    U --> A[React Frontend]
    
    A --> B[Authentication Layer]
    A --> C[Supabase Client]
    
    B --> D[Supabase Auth]
    C --> E[Supabase API]
    
    E --> F[(PostgreSQL Database)]
    E --> G[Row-Level Security]
    E --> H[Supabase Storage]
    E --> I[Realtime]
    
    J[Supabase Edge Functions] --> D
    J --> F
```

## Frontend Layer

Built using:

* React
* Vite
* TailwindCSS
* Lucide Icons

The frontend is divided into reusable components, pages, hooks, query modules, and utility functions.

## Backend Layer

Supabase provides:

* PostgreSQL database
* Authentication
* Row-Level Security
* Storage
* Realtime subscriptions
* Edge Functions

## Security Layer

Authorization is enforced at the database level through PostgreSQL RLS policies rather than relying exclusively on frontend checks.

This means a malicious user cannot gain additional database access simply by manipulating frontend JavaScript or browser requests.

